WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.770
The Brother KL 116 Knitleader is a full-

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:11.380
size charting device that's extremely
handy for shaping your knits visually.

3
00:00:11.410 --> 00:00:12.980
With just a few steps,

4
00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:17.660
you'll be creating your own knitting
patterns with very little math.

5
00:00:17.690 --> 00:00:23.360
Here's a listing of machines
that this device can be used with.

6
00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:30.380
The key to using a knitleader  is a Swatch.

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.620
For this device, it's critical that you
knit and measure a Swatch 40 stitches

8
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.340
by 60 rows and measure
in the middle of the Swatch.

9
00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:46.660
This is a proper swatch with yarn markers
indicating the stitches in the middle

10
00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:52.500
of the piece and contrasting rows
marking the measurement of the rows.

11
00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:59.460
You can use the metric ruler included with
the knitleader or a standard metric ruler.

12
00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:04.570
Record the measurement
of 40 stitches and 60 rows.

13
00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.130
Next, establish the piece that you'll be

14
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:13.380
knitting and draw or copy a full
size piece for your knitting.

15
00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:17.340
In this example, I'm copying
a sleeve from a sewing pattern.

16
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:20.260
There was a special pen or a pencil

17
00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.140
that came with the machine,
and I'm not sure if these still exist,

18
00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:30.180
but you can test other methods of marking
the Mylar sheet for the knitleader.

19
00:01:30.210 --> 00:01:32.740
Just Mark in the lower corner of the sheet

20
00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:35.900
and test that you can
easily remove the marks.

21
00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:40.100
You're going to reuse
the Mylar over and over.

22
00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.260
I'm just using a soft pencil.

23
00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.780
I found it came off pretty easily.

24
00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:50.280
I didn't have any tuck with a dry
erase marker, so be careful.

25
00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:55.460
I'll use the sleeve pattern
for the next steps.

26
00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.660
Notice that I've lined up the center

27
00:01:57.690 --> 00:02:01.740
of my sleeve along the center
dotted line of the sheet.

28
00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:07.100
This will position the knitting
in the middle of the knitting machine.

29
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.260
You'll want to consider the position

30
00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:12.580
of your stitch pattern
in relation to your knitting.

31
00:02:12.610 --> 00:02:14.890
Remember, punch card machines will

32
00:02:14.920 --> 00:02:19.340
automatically position the stitch
pattern in the middle of the bed.

33
00:02:19.370 --> 00:02:22.040
Plan ahead where you want to position your

34
00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:25.610
stitch pattern based on the
machine that you're using.

35
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:30.060
Next, attach the knitleader
to your knitting machine.

36
00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.140
Slip the setting plates into the slots

37
00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:39.080
at the back of the machine
and slide the knitleader into position so

38
00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:44.860
that the feeding lever engages
with the knitleader row counter tripper.

39
00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:46.540
Now, this is a separate tripper

40
00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:53.300
from the regular machine row counter
tripper. I found on my 260 machine.

41
00:02:53.330 --> 00:03:00.760
I had to remove the accessory cover so
that feeding lever could move cleanly.

42
00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:05.540
Once you have the machine set
up the knitleader in place,

43
00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:08.420
swatch knitted,

44
00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.940
your piece drawn on the Mylar,

45
00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.340
there are a few steps for putting the knitleader

46
00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:19.160
to work to create
the knitting instructions.

47
00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.660
This part of the knitleader
device is called the regulator.

48
00:03:23.690 --> 00:03:29.460
It's a mechanical device
that advances the sheet as you knit.

49
00:03:29.490 --> 00:03:36.500
The regulator is set according to your
gauge and advances the sheet accurately.

50
00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:40.860
For example, if my gauge is 28 rows per

51
00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:45.060
ten centimeters,
setting the regulator correctly,

52
00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:51.500
the sheet will advance 28 times
for every 10 CM of my sleeve.

53
00:03:51.530 --> 00:03:55.220
If my gauge is 15 rows per 10 centimeters,

54
00:03:55.250 --> 00:04:03.080
the regulator will advance 15 times
for every 10 CM of my knitted piece.

55
00:04:03.160 --> 00:04:09.180
The dial on the left allows you
to manually advance or move back rows,

56
00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:15.180
so if you make a mistake and rip out
a few rows, you can move the row

57
00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:19.360
count back on the knitleader.

58
00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.860
To set the device to your gauge,
push in and hold the clutch button

59
00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:32.560
and slide the upper and lower
indicators to match your gauge.

60
00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.620
This setting indicates a row gauge

61
00:04:47.650 --> 00:04:56.220
of 10.0 CM, and this is a gauge of 20.8

62
00:04:56.250 --> 00:05:00.120
so you set both of the slides.

63
00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:03.440
Remember, the regulator controls

64
00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:08.380
the advancement of the sheet
and reflects the row gauge.

65
00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:14.140
I always think of regulator
R and R for row.

66
00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:18.380
For the stitch gauge,
use the stitch measure scales.

67
00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:22.140
Stitch - scales. The chart in the manual

68
00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:26.540
and the chart that's attached
to the tubular sheet case help you

69
00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:32.800
identify which scale to use
based on your stitch gauge.

70
00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:41.740
If 40 stitches in your Swatch measures 108
mm or 10.8 CM, you would use the

71
00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:46.820
#8 scale aligned to the side
that is marked 108.

72
00:05:46.850 --> 00:05:53.740
Insert the scale in the knitleader
so that 108 side is showing.

73
00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:58.140
If 40 stitches measures 12.5 CM,

74
00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:06.880
use the green #12 scale
with the 125 side showing.

75
00:06:08.200 --> 00:06:09.260
This example,

76
00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:13.860
using this scale, I would have
64 stitches on the needlebed.

77
00:06:13.890 --> 00:06:18.100
Using this scale, there would
be 120 stitches in work.

78
00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.660
Big difference.

79
00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:23.860
This is the beauty of using
a charting device.

80
00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:29.660
You can draw your pieces and use them
again and again with different yarns,

81
00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:34.720
different stitch patterns,
and even a different knitting machine.

82
00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.660
Make sure you have measured
your gauge correctly.

83
00:06:38.690 --> 00:06:41.180
Set the regulator rows correctly

84
00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:48.400
and selected the correct stitch measure
scales and you're ready to get knitting.

85
00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:51.140
Using the device is visual.

86
00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:54.420
Simply follow the lines
as you're knitting.

87
00:06:54.450 --> 00:06:59.580
The numbers on the scale represent
the needles on the needlebed.

88
00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:02.740
When your drawn line aligns with a number

89
00:07:02.770 --> 00:07:09.380
on the scale, you will increase,
decrease, cast on or bind off stitches.

90
00:07:09.410 --> 00:07:13.140
In other words, you'll
be shaping your piece.

91
00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:18.280
Use your judgment when
the line is between a number.

92
00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.240
For example, at this point of shaping

93
00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:29.320
my sleeve cap, needles left 31
through right 30 should be in work.

94
00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:37.340
At this point it would
be left 38 and right 37.

95
00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.900
But I would probably choose to knit
my sleeve cap to be symmetrical.

96
00:07:42.920 --> 00:07:47.540
Left 38, right 38, left 37, right 37.

97
00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.460
But it's really up to you.

98
00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:55.520
Keep following the line
to shape your garment piece.

99
00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:58.300
Now here's a tip for symmetry.

100
00:07:58.330 --> 00:08:00.380
Just draw half of the design.

101
00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:04.440
In my case, draw half of the sleeve if

102
00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:08.380
it's symmetrical and then
mirror the shaping.

103
00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:12.340
So this works for sleeves
and some garment pieces.

104
00:08:12.360 --> 00:08:18.540
Another trick is to draw one body
shape with multiple necklines.

105
00:08:18.570 --> 00:08:22.260
So here you would have the back neckline.

106
00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:27.300
You could also have your Vneck
line on the same body shape.

107
00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:29.060
It would also speed up the process

108
00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:36.880
of drawing and add to accuracy your
fronts and your back would match.

109
00:08:37.160 --> 00:08:44.020
Another use for a knitleader is to use
it for manually creating stitch patterns.

110
00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:47.060
Think about drawing an Intarsia design right

111
00:08:47.080 --> 00:08:55.240
on the sheet and follow row by row
changing colors as Indicated

112
00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:57.860
once you understand how it works,

113
00:08:57.890 --> 00:09:00.700
this model of knitleader is an easy-

114
00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:06.040
to-use device that can help you quickly
create full-size knitting patterns.

